contentText:
It’s a daunting task that takes place at every major U.S. sporting event: Before any athlete hits the field, a performer sings “The Star-Spangled Banner.”&nbsp;

There have been iconic, inspirational versions of The National Anthem, like Whitney Houston ’s soaring rendering at the 1991 Super Bowl, which stands out as the definitive version, followed closely by Lady Gaga ’s towering take at the 2016 Super Bowl and Chris Stapleton ’s soulful rendition at the 2023 game. There are also ones that didn’t fare as well and show why it can be a risky endeavor for an artist to attempt, such as Fergie ’s questionable interpretation at the 2020 NBA All-Star Game or Steven Tyler ’s overwrought go at the 2012 AFC Championship. It’s a high-profile, often nerve-wracking assignment that, these days, is immediately lionized or demonized via social media and lives online forever.

