With the NBA Finals, the World Cup and the MLB All-Star Game all occurring within recent weeks, a smorgasbord of popular artists — including soulful pop singer Tori Kelly , multi-platinum country-pop duo Dan+ Shay , powerful country/R&B performer Mickey Guyton , R&B legend Patti LaBelle and Oscar winner Jennifer Hudson — have all delivered stirring renditions of The National Anthem.

“Singing the National Anthem at the World Cup was one of the [most] surreal experiences of our entire career,” says Dan + Shay’s Dan Smyers, noting the June 12 World Cup match between the U.S. and Paraguay was the first time they performed the song, despite having been asked previously. Adding to the nerves is the event’s high celebrity quotient. As the pair were singing a cappella, they looked up at the Jumbotron to see Tom Cruise standing with David Beckham and singing along. “I saw them and almost lost it,” Smyers says.&nbsp;&nbsp;

So how do major sporting events find the right match? When looking for a performer, it is no surprise that vocal ability “has got to be at the top of the list,” says Seth Dudowsky , the National Football League’s former head of music who is now executive vp/head of sports and live content at Jesse Collins Entertainment. JCE produces major television events, including entertainment for sporting events like the Super Bowl. “It’s one of the hardest songs there is to sing, and it’s also one [where] artists need to feel confident going in. Depending upon when and where it is, the Anthem is a great way for leagues and events to be able to tie in local representation or memorialize a holiday or anniversary.” For example, JCE picked hometown girl LaBelle to perform at the July 14 game in Philadelphia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

“When we look for a National Anthem singer, whether it’s the Super Bowl or another tentpole like the Draft, we’re looking for an artist who can bring a fresh, authentic perspective to the song while still honoring its tradition. It must feel true to who they are as an artist but also stand up to the magnitude of the moment,” says Tim Tubito , the NFL’s senior director of global event presentation, music & entertainment.&nbsp; “If you look at recent Super Bowl performances — Charlie Puth , Jon Batiste , Reba , Chris Stapleton — each approached it differently, but all delivered something emotional, memorable and distinctly their own.”

Inside a Top Voice Coach&#8217;s Work With Ariana Grande, Rosalía and Hollywood’s Biggest&nbsp;Biopics

Though there’s no formal list, entertainment execs at major sporting leagues and producers informally trade notes about which artists are particularly strong candidates. For example, “ The War and Treaty to me have shown time and time again they’re among the best,” Dudowsky says of the duo who performed the National Anthem at the 2026 Fiesta Bowl and one of the 2023 Thanksgiving NFL games. “They’ve done many different types of arrangements, and they’re a husband-and-wife team with a military background.”&nbsp;

That kind of reputation tends to come up in discussions. “We have strong relationships across sports and entertainment, and there’s a sharing of ideas and best practices around major events. That goes beyond the Anthem; it’s about the full presentation,” says Tubito. “At the same time, we’re always watching. Whether it’s another league, an awards show, or a global event like the World Cup, we’re paying attention to performances that break through. Those moments can absolutely put an artist on the radar.”

While no policy dictates it, the singer is usually a U.S. citizen, which is why powerhouse vocalists like Celine Dion and Michael Bublé — both of whom are Canadian — haven’t taken on the Anthem (though Dion did sing “God Bless America” at the 2003 Super Bowl).

“We generally prefer the performer to be a citizen of the country whose anthem they’re singing because it adds a level of personal connection and authenticity to the performance,” Tubito says.

The knowledge of who’s a proven performer comes in handy when singers are needed on short notice, such as when a best-of-seven playoff series goes beyond four games. This year, during the NBA Finals, Guyton was put on hold for a week and confirmed to sing for Game 5 once Game 4 was over.

Country Music Artists Who Have Performed the National Anthem at the Super&nbsp;Bowl

Guyton was a known quantity, having sung the National Anthem at the 2022 Super Bowl and other sporting events. “There is a little bit of a circuit for the Anthem, especially for the big events. [The leagues] so desperately want to make sure that there&#8217;s going to be a good performance and there&#8217;s not going to be negative headlines,” says Guyton’s manager, Cameo Carlson . “Once you&#8217;ve delivered one solid [rendition] at a big event, you&#8217;re sort of in the loop on this.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

Similarly, Kelly, who sang the National Anthem at the first game of this year’s NBA Finals on June 3, has a proven track record. She’s performed the anthem at the 2021 Kentucky Derby and at the 2024 Mike Tyson-Jake Paul boxing match. Her manager, Alex Silverman , says the NBA talent coordinators were the ones who approached her.

The National Anthem is notoriously hard to sing, and only a finite number of artists have the chops. “I think what makes it more difficult than people realize is there&#8217;s a lot of held notes,” Eric Vetro , vocal coach for Puth, Ariana Grande and Sabrina Carpenter , among others, said on Billboard ’s On the Record podcast in June . “For someone who&#8217;s been on Broadway or grown up with that type of music, holding long notes is all part of it. But most pop songs don&#8217;t have a lot of long-held notes, or notes that might have a lot of vibrato in them. ‘The Star-Spangled Banner,’ the highest notes are held for quite a long time, and that partly makes it difficult.”&nbsp;

There are also other factors, such as dealing with the echo and reverb from the venue and the pressure of performing before a huge crowd that is not there primarily to see you, Vento says.&nbsp;

&#8220;As a management company, we would tell 99% of our artists probably to pass on the National Anthem, just because there is usually so little upside and so much potential downside if you don’t do a great job,” says Kevin “Chief” Zaruk , who manages Dan + Shay with Simon Tikhman . “Truthfully, most artists don’t want to do it. It’s just an immense amount of pressure.”

